#
# FINDINGS THAT WERE FIXED, NOT ACCEPTED.
#
# `.gitleaks.toml` says a value is not a credential and never will be. This file
# says something different and much narrower: a value WAS in the history, it has
# been removed, and the commit that carried it is still inside a range the
# scanner reads. One line here is one finding — commit, file, rule and line — so
# it can never suppress anything but the exact occurrence it names. The same
# string in a new commit is a new finding, which is the property that makes this
# the right instrument rather than an allowlist.
#
# Nothing that is still in the tree belongs here. If a value is live, remove it
# and rotate it; an entry below would be hiding it.

# `a-third-passphrase-entirely-1` in `PasswordResetIT`, 2026-09-15.
#
# A second passphrase invented for one test — the test signs in, resets, and has
# to prove the OLD password no longer works, so it needs two distinct values.
# Inventing the second one was the mistake `.gitleaks.toml` already describes one
# class of: a bespoke password that happens not to trip today. This one tripped
# immediately, and `0b65b21` replaced it with a value derived from the single
# allowlisted passphrase, which is what the second value should always have been.
#
# It is listed here rather than allowlisted because allowlisting it would
# re-permit exactly the habit that produced it, and rather than rewritten out of
# the branch because a shared branch's history is not edited to make a scanner
# quiet. It was never a credential: no account outside a Testcontainers run ever
# had it, and that database is destroyed with the run.
